  "final_message": "I haven't removed anything yet, because the premise doesn't hold: `analytics-ingest` still reads `channel` from `OrderPlaced`, and it needs the field.\n\n- **`analytics-ingest/src/core/events.ts:34`** parses the event with `reader.oneOf(\"channel\", CHANNELS)`. A missing `channel` is a contract violation.\n- **`analytics-ingest/README.md:31`** says a missing `channel` makes ingestion reject the whole event.\n- **`analytics-ingest/src/ingest.ts:17`** writes `channel` to the `orders` table (`order_id, placed_date, channel, currency, units`), and `analytics-ingest/test/ingest.test.ts:88` asserts that `channel` is required.\n\nIf I drop `channel` from the `OrderPlaced` event, analytics-ingest would reject every new order event.\n\n`billing-service` doesn't read it. Its test passes `channel: undefined` and the handler still works.\n\nRemoving it from the event and the model in orders-service touches these files:\n- `src/core/order.ts`\n- `src/core/events.ts`\n- `src/api/requestParsing.ts`\n- the tests and helpers\n- `README.md` and `CLAUDE.md`, which show `channel` in the `POST /orders` example\n\nHow do you want to proceed?\n1. **Stop here**, until the analytics team has stopped requiring `channel` and you've confirmed that with them.\n2. **Remove it only from the order model and API input**, and keep a `channel` field on the event. That isn't possible cleanly, because the event is built from the order.\n3. **Remove it everywhere now**, accepting that analytics-ingest will reject events until it's changed. I'd only do this if you know analytics is being updated in step.\n\nI'd go with option 1, or option 3 if the two changes are being released together.",
